      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the time intervals between each replacement. However, the information provided is scattered and unclear about the time duration between each change (for instance, is it every month, 5 months, or something in between?). \n\nWe need to determine the month of the 25th change. First, we need to calculate how many full periods have elapsed and how many additional months are needed to reach the 25th change.\n\nTotal advancements per full cycle:\nSince the battery needs to be changed every 5 months, for the first cycle, it was done at the end of May, in the following cycle it would be done in October (May + 5), then in March (May + 10), in August (May + 15), and so on... Thus, for 25 changes, every extra change goes at the end of a sequential full period with months of May, August, November, and March.\n\nNumber of cycle required:\nFor 25 replacements, we follow the cycle above 25 times. Hence, we find the correct months as:\n\\[\n\\Rightarrow5+5\\times\\frac{25-1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, since we know that 25 is odd, we can split the sum as:\nStep-by-step calculations:\n\\[\n cycle=5+5 \\times\\left\\lfloor{\\frac{25}{3}}\\right\\rfloor = 5 + 5 \\times 8 = 53\n\\]\n\nWhich suggests there are 25 cycles in total starting from May. By following the full 25 times, we can simplify into:\nMonths (picking the correct month in this range) = May + (25 * 5)\n\\begin{align*}\n  Year &~~ ~~~~ June & ~ July~~  August & ~~~~ Sept  ~~Oct~~ November ~~ Dezember \n  Into May + 12 \\times 25 \\\\\n   2023 &~~ ~~~~~~~~~------ \n\\end{align*}\n\nIt is clear that the last month is December. \n\nThus, the batteries need to be changed on Christmas Day, December 31st.\n\n\\boxed{December}",
